      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The aim of the present comparative, non-randomized intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) study was to test the effect of pravastatin on late neointima formation in stented de novo lesions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The treatment group consisted of 28 consecutive patients in whom 31 stents were deployed; all patients were prescribed 40 mg daily of pravastatin for a mean follow-up period of 14 +/- 3 months (group 1). The control group consisted of 27 consecutive patients in whom 30 stents were deployed; lipid-lowering treatment was not prescribed; the mean follow-up period for this group of patients was 13 +/- 3 months (group 2). At follow-up IVUS images were acquired at a continuous 0.5 mm/s speed. IVUS measurements of the lumen area, stent area and neointima area were calculated within the stent at 0.5 mm intervals."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The stent dimensions and technique of implantation were similar in the two groups. At follow-up the minimal lumen diameter at quantitative coronary angiography was slightly larger in group 1 than in group 2 (2.43 +/- 0.58 vs 2.17 +/- 0.59 mm, p = NS), while the late loss tended to be lower in group 1 than in group 2 (0.28 +/- 0.39 vs 0.63 +/- 0.37 mm, p = NS). At IVUS evaluation, the lumen and stent areas were similar in the two groups whereas the percent neointima area was significantly lower in group 1 than in group 2 (21 +/- 11 vs 29 +/- 11% respectively, p < 0.03)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Pravastatin treatment was associated with a significantly reduced late in-stent neointima formation as assessed at IVUS."
        }
      ]},
